-- Passage rg11-text-structure-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ed98755c-e69f-54ac-a211-c5acb5753535', 'rg11-text-structure-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-text-structure'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Painting the Roofs White$q$, $q$On the hottest afternoon of last July, a thermometer on a black tar roof in the warehouse district of Calder Bay read 79 degrees Celsius. A few blocks away, on the shaded lawn of Linden Park, the air sat at 33 degrees. That gap is not a curiosity. It is a warning about how our city is built, and it points toward one of the cheapest fixes available to us.

Dark roofs absorb most of the sunlight that strikes them and release that energy as heat, both into the buildings below and into the surrounding air. Multiply one roof by the roughly forty thousand flat roofs in Calder Bay, and the result is a city that stays several degrees warmer than the countryside long after sunset. Residents feel it in their electric bills, and older residents feel it in their health, because nights that never cool down give the body little chance to recover.

The remedy is almost embarrassingly simple: coat those roofs with a reflective white material. A pilot program run by the city housing office last summer treated 120 apartment buildings on the east side. Over the following three months, top-floor units in those buildings averaged indoor temperatures 3 degrees lower than similar units in untreated buildings, and cooling costs fell by about one fifth.

Critics raise two fair objections. Some worry that white coatings must be cleaned and reapplied, adding a recurring expense. Others point out that a bright roof reflects heat away in winter too, when residents might welcome it. Both concerns are real, but neither is decisive. The coatings tested in the pilot lasted eight years before needing renewal, and the mild winters of Calder Bay mean the lost warmth is small compared with the summer savings.

The city council will vote next month on whether to expand the pilot to every public building. It should vote yes, and then offer grants so that private owners can follow. A city that waits for a perfect solution will spend many more summers reading numbers like 79 on its rooftop thermometers.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
where public.passages.status = 'draft';
