-- Passage rg11-text-structure-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('e6c0f0f7-b15b-56bb-bf59-97e89cf9457a', 'rg11-text-structure-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-text-structure'), null,
        '9-12', $q$When Every Town Kept Its Own Time$q$, $q$Two hundred years ago, the towns along the Vesner River each set their clocks by the sun. When the sun stood highest over the market square, the town clock was set to noon. Because the sun reaches its highest point a little later as one travels west, the clock in Ardmoor, the easternmost town, ran about eleven minutes ahead of the clock in Pell Crossing at the western end of the valley. For centuries, no one minded. A traveler on horseback covered so little ground in a day that a few minutes of difference went unnoticed.

The railway changed that. When the Vesner Valley line opened, a train could cross the whole valley in under three hours, and its timetable had to tell passengers when it would reach each stop. Printers tried listing the railway time and each local time side by side, but the double columns confused travelers, and stationmasters complained that passengers missed trains while arguing about which clock to trust.

In response, the railway company adopted a single time for the entire line, based on the clock in Ardmoor, where its offices stood. A telegraph signal was sent down the wires each morning so that every station clock could be corrected to match. At first, many towns kept their own clocks running beside the railway clocks, and some town halls even displayed two minute hands on the same face.

Within a generation, the old local times faded. Merchants found it easier to schedule deliveries by railway time, schools began ringing their bells by it, and the town councils, one after another, voted to adopt it. By the time the council in Pell Crossing finally agreed, few residents could remember setting a clock by the noon sun.

The story shows how a technology can quietly reshape something as basic as the hour of the day. No law forced the change at first. A practical problem on the railway produced a solution that spread, step by step, until it simply became normal.$q$, 'draft')
