-- Passage rg11-text-structure-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('666d1a89-66e8-5116-a9b9-7bf4c231c3c5', 'rg11-text-structure-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-text-structure'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Do Visitors Remember More When They Listen?$q$, $q$Museums spend large sums on audio guides, the recorded tours that visitors play through headphones as they walk from exhibit to exhibit. But does listening help people learn more than simply reading the printed labels on the walls? A team of researchers at the fictional Solberg Institute set out to answer that question at a natural history museum in their city.

The researchers recruited 240 adult visitors and divided them at random into two groups. Both groups toured the same gallery of fossils for thirty minutes. One group used an audio guide, and the other read the wall labels, which contained the same information word for word. As visitors left, each one answered a short quiz about the exhibits, and two weeks later the researchers phoned every participant to give a second quiz.

On the quiz taken at the exit, the two groups scored almost the same. The difference appeared later. Two weeks after their visit, the audio group remembered noticeably more, answering on average about two more questions correctly out of fifteen. Visitors in the audio group also spent more time looking directly at the fossils, while label readers divided their attention between the objects and the walls.

The researchers caution against drawing broad conclusions. The study took place in a single gallery, with adult visitors only, and the recordings were narrated by a skilled actor whose voice may have made the information more memorable than an ordinary recording would. They plan to repeat the study with children and in an art museum.

Still, the findings suggest a practical lesson. If a museum wants lasting memory rather than quick recall, the eyes may matter as much as the ears: a guide that frees visitors to keep looking at the objects may well be worth its cost.$q$, 'draft')
